**Chaptarr version:** 0.9.936 (develop branch, as of 2026-08-26)

**Bug:** `PUT /api/v1/author/{id}` silently clears `Path`, `AudiobookRootFolderPath`, and `EbookRootFolderPath` on the stored author whenever a request simply omits those fields - there's no way to distinguish "client didn't send this field" from "client explicitly wants it cleared."

**Root cause:** `AuthorResource.ToModel(resource, facadeContext)` (`src/Chaptarr.Api.V1/Author/AuthorResource.cs`) builds a fresh model from the resource, and for the native (non-facade) branch, an omitted `AudiobookRootFolderPath` (and no legacy `RootFolderPath` fallback either) resolves to `null`. `Author.ApplyChanges` (`src/NzbDrone.Core/Books/Model/Author.cs`, ~line 274 onward) then copies `Path`, `AudiobookRootFolderPath`, and `EbookRootFolderPath` from that built model onto the STORED author **unconditionally** - including copying `null` over whatever was already there:

```csharp
Path = other.Path;
...
AudiobookRootFolderPath = other.AudiobookRootFolderPath;
EbookRootFolderPath = other.EbookRootFolderPath;
```

This is inconsistent with how the same method treats the four tri-state monitoring fields two lines later, which are only copied "when explicitly provided (not null)" specifically to avoid this exact problem:

```csharp
// TRI-STATE MONITORING SYSTEM - Copy only when explicitly provided (not null).
// This prevents partial updates from wiping existing monitoring values.
if (other.AudiobookMonitorExisting.HasValue) { ... }
```

**Reproduction:**
1. An author has `audiobookRootFolderPath: "/audiobooks"` and `ebookRootFolderPath: "/ebooks"` set (confirm via `GET /api/v1/author/{id}`).
2. `PUT /api/v1/author/{id}` with a body that includes only the fields you actually mean to change - e.g. just `{"id": 123, "monitored": false}` (plus whatever `Path`/quality-profile-id fields the endpoint's own validators require to accept the request at all).
3. `GET /api/v1/author/{id}` again: both `audiobookRootFolderPath` and `ebookRootFolderPath` are now `null`, along with `path`. Nothing about the request asked for that.

**Why this isn't a trivial one-line fix:** the obvious fix - mirror the tri-state fields' "only copy when non-null" pattern - breaks a legitimate use of the current behavior. `BookController.cs` (~line 1755-1779, in its Readarr/Seerr facade normalization for adding a book) deliberately does:

```csharp
else
{
    author.AudiobookRootFolderPath = null;
    author.AudiobookMetadataProfileId = null;
}
```

on an `AuthorResource` it's about to hand off through the same `ToModel`/`ApplyChanges` path, relying on that `null` actually propagating through to clear the stored value. A plain nullable `string` field can't currently distinguish "the request explicitly wants this cleared" from "the request just didn't mention it" - both look identical (`null`) by the time `ApplyChanges` runs, so a change that fixes the reported bug would silently break that intentional-clear code path instead.

A real fix probably needs some form of presence-tracking at the JSON binding layer (e.g. only including a field in the resource if it was actually present in the request body, rather than relying on C# `null` to mean two different things), or an explicit way for a caller like `BookController` to signal "clear this" that doesn't rely on `ApplyChanges`'s current unconditional-copy behavior for these three fields specifically.

**How this was found:** flagged as a known, deliberately-left-open gap while working on PR #87 (`PUT monitored:false` not persisting - a related but distinct bug, since fixed there). That PR's cascade logic has to work around this exact behavior (snapshotting root folder paths from the stored author *before* `ApplyChanges` runs, rather than trusting the post-mutation value) rather than being able to fix it, since fixing it is out of that PR's scope.

Happy to help track down the least-invasive way to resolve the ambiguity if that'd be useful - didn't want to guess at architectural direction (presence-tracking vs. a dedicated "clear" signal vs. something else) without your input.

Chaptarr version 0.9.958.0 (develop, Docker, linux).

To be clear up front: this is **not** a re-open of #112. I closed that one myself after tracing the code, because ebook colocation relocating an ebook into the audiobook's folder is the `PlaceEbooksWithAudiobooks` feature working exactly as designed. This is a separate, narrower defect in the same handler that I found while tracing a later relocation: `EbookColocateOnAudiobookImportHandler` moves ebook files even when the colocation planner has **declined** to produce a colocated destination.

## The defect

`EbookColocateOnAudiobookImportHandler.Handle` does this per sibling ebook file:

    var plan = _bookFileMover.GetOrganizeDestination(ebookFile, author, false);
    if (!plan.CanOrganize)
    {
        _logger.Warn("Skipping ebook colocation for {0}: {1}", previousPath, plan.SkipReason);
        continue;
    }

    _bookFileMover.MoveBookFile(ebookFile, author, plan);

`BookFileMovingService.GetOrganizeDestination` builds the plain naming destination first, then asks the colocation planner and overrides the destination only if the planner applies:

    var newFileName = _buildFileNames.BuildBookFileName(author, edition, bookFile, namingConfig);
    var destinationPath = Path.Combine(destinationAuthorFolderPath, newFileName + extension);
    var colocationPlan = _ebookColocationPlanner.Plan(bookFile, author, edition, fileNameOnly, renameBatchContext);

    if (colocationPlan.Applies)
    {
        destinationPath = colocationPlan.PrimaryPath;
        ...
    }

    return new BookFileMovePlan { CanOrganize = true, ... };

`CanOrganize` is hardcoded true outside the `Applies` branch, and the returned plan carries no signal of which of the two destinations it holds. So when the planner declines, the handler still calls `MoveBookFile` and relocates the ebook to its plain canonical naming path. That is a full re-organize of a file that was never part of the triggering audiobook import, performed by a handler whose only mandate is colocation.

## Why the planner can decline while the handler proceeds

The two gates read **different root folders**.

The handler resolves the root from the audiobook's quality:

    var rootFolderPath = author.GetRootFolderForQuality(imported.Quality.Quality);
    var bestRoot = _rootFolderService.GetBestRootFolder(rootFolderPath);
    if (bestRoot == null || bestRoot.FolderType != FolderType.Mixed || !bestRoot.PlaceEbooksWithAudiobooks)
    {
        return;
    }

`EbookColocationPlanner.Plan` resolves the root from the ebook's quality:

    var rootFolderPath = author.GetRootFolderForQuality(bookFile.Quality.Quality);
    var bestRoot = _rootFolderService.GetBestRootFolder(rootFolderPath);
    if (bestRoot == null || bestRoot.FolderType != FolderType.Mixed || !bestRoot.PlaceEbooksWithAudiobooks)
    {
        return EbookColocationPlan.Skipped(EbookColocationSkipReason.RootNotMixedOrDisabled, cleanupReplicas: true);
    }

`Author.GetRootFolderForQuality` returns `EbookRootFolderPath` or `AudiobookRootFolderPath` depending on the media type of the quality, so these are genuinely different roots for an author configured with split roots.

That configuration is supported and passes validation: `AuthorService.IsCompatibleRootFolder` accepts a Mixed root for either media type and an Ebook root for ebooks. And `RootFolderResource` forces `PlaceEbooksWithAudiobooks = false` whenever `FolderType != Mixed`, so an author with `AudiobookRootFolderPath` on a Mixed root and `EbookRootFolderPath` on an ebook only root is **guaranteed** to pass the handler's gate and fail the planner's. `NoAudiobookFolders` is reachable the same way, when the audiobook's files are not under the ebook's root.

`TryGetPhysicalAuthorFolder` does not short circuit this: it is called with the ebook's own root and the ebook's current path, so for an ebook sitting normally under its ebook root it succeeds and execution reaches the planner.

## Secondary symptom: one already-correct ebook aborts the whole batch

In that same declined state, `MoveBookFile` takes its non colocation branch and calls `TransferFile`, which throws on a no-op move:

    if (bookFilePath == destinationFilePath)
    {
        throw new SameFilenameException("File not moved, source and destination are the same", bookFilePath);
    }

The handler's `try/catch` wraps the entire method body, outside both `foreach` loops, and `SameFilenameException` is caught nowhere in this path (`RenameBookFileService` is the only place in the codebase that catches it). So if an ebook is already at its canonical path, the whole batch aborts with the generic "Failed to colocate ebooks after audiobook import" warning and every remaining ebook file for that work is skipped. `CleanupReplicaFilesIfAny` and `EnsureBookFolder` have already run by then, so side effects land before the abort.

## Expected vs actual

Expected: a handler whose sole purpose is to colocate ebooks with audiobooks should not move anything when there is no colocated destination to move to.

Actual: it relocates the file to the plain naming path, or throws and aborts the remainder of the batch.

## How I found it

An epub that Chaptarr had adopted from a disk scan (not downloaded) at `/books/<Author>/<Title>/` was relocated into the audiobook's `{Book Series}/{Book Title}/` folder during an unrelated audiobook import, leaving an external library scanner pointing at a path that no longer existed. The relocation itself is the colocation feature behaving correctly, per #112. The `History` row for it, on the **ebook's** book row rather than the imported audiobook's:

    Id: 1274
    BookId: 29365            (ebook row; the audiobook imported was book 29294)
    EventType: 6             (BookFileRenamed)
    Date: 2026-09-02 01:47:41.4207014
    Data: {"sourcePath":"/books/<Author>/<Title>/<Title> - <Author>.epub",
           "path":"/books/<Author>/<Series>/<Title>/<Title> - <Author>.epub",
           "size":"660097"}

timestamped 3.6ms before the audiobook's own `BookFileImported` row, which is the handler running as an `IHandle<TrackImportedEvent>` and publishing `BookFileRenamedEvent` before `HistoryService` records the import. Reading that handler to confirm the mechanism is what surfaced the missing check above.

One small related note while I am here: on a default Info install nothing at all is logged when this handler moves a file. The move is logged only via `_logger.Debug("Co-located ebook after audiobook import: ...")`, and `BookFileMovingService` logs at Debug too, so the only Info level trace that a file moved is the `BookFileRenamed` History row. An Info line when the handler relocates a file would have made the above much easier to spot.

## Repro

I could not reproduce the declined-planner path against my own live instance without reconfiguring my root folders, so I pinned it with a unit test instead: a handler test that hands the handler a plan where colocation did not apply and asserts no move happens. It fails on current `develop` and passes with the fix. I have a small fix with tests and will open a PR referencing this issue.

Happy to provide more detail on any of the above.

## Summary

Clicking "Rescan Folders" for a single author (or triggering a `RefreshAuthor` command with `rescanFolders: true` for one `authorId`) does **not** actually scope the scan to that author. It walks every root folder in the library instead, and only *labels* itself with the author's name in the UI.

## Root cause

In `RefreshAuthorService.Rescan()`:

```csharp
var rootFolders = _rootFolderService.All();
// ...filtered by mediaType only, never by author...
var folders = rootFolders.Select(x => x.Path).ToList();

var command = new RescanFoldersCommand(folders, FilterFilesType.Matched, authorIds)
```

`folders` is built from **every** root folder regardless of `authorIds`. Downstream, `DiskScanService.Execute(RescanFoldersCommand)` → `Scan(folders, filter, authorIds, ...)` only uses `authorIds` for one thing: firing the "scan completed" notification event at the very end. It plays no role in deciding what actually gets walked (`DiskScanService.cs`, `Scan()`, ~line 97-236).

## Impact

On a library with several thousand author folders (mine has ~9,200), a "single-author" rescan silently becomes a full synchronous library walk — each folder processed via a blocking `orchestratorTask.Wait(cancellationToken)` inside the scan loop. Because `RescanFoldersCommand.RequiresDiskAccess = true` and disk-access commands appear to serialize on a shared lock/queue, this also stalls every other queued command (imports, other refreshes) for the entire duration — observed taking well over an hour and, from a user's perspective, indistinguishable from a hang.

This reproduced consistently across different authors on our instance, and matches the "adding an author takes 5 minutes" / "everything stalls behind Rescan Folders" reports we'd been chasing.

## Fix

When `authorIds` is non-empty, scope `folders` to the affected authors' own `AudiobookPath`/`EbookPath` instead of every root folder. Falls back to the existing full root-folder scan only when no authors are specified (e.g. a library-wide refresh) or none have a resolvable path yet.

```csharp
var folders = GetAuthorScopedRescanFolders(authorIds, requestedMediaType);

if (folders.Count == 0)
{
    // existing full root-folder fallback
}
```

## Testing

Verified live against a ~9,200-author library (Docker build from this branch):

- Before: `RescanFolders` command body showed `folders` = full root folder list; the corresponding command sat in `started` for well over an hour before being manually cancelled, blocking everything queued behind it.
- After: triggering `RefreshAuthor` (`authorId`, `rescanFolders: true`) for a single author now produces a `RescanFolders` command with `folders: ["/audiobooks/<AuthorName>"]`, and it completes in under 2 minutes.

Happy to add a fixture to `DiskScanServiceFixture.cs` / a service test for `RefreshAuthorService` covering this if useful — wanted to get eyes on the root cause and approach first.

## The bug

`TryRecoverRestrictedMissViaV5` bails out as soon as `allowAuthorImport` is false:

```csharp
if (!allowAuthorImport)
{
    return (null, suggestion, s.Reason);
}

var recoveredAuthor = TryGetOrImportSuggestedAuthorForRestrictedRecovery(...);
```

The existing-author lookup (`FindByProviderId`) lives inside `TryGetOrImportSuggestedAuthorForRestrictedRecovery`, below the early return - so any matching context with `AllowAuthorImport = false` (notably `MatchingContextPresets.ForDownloaded`, i.e. every completed-download import) can never link a V5 suggestion to an author **already in the library**. The file is rejected with `No match in local library. Suggested author: <name> (<providerId>)` even when that exact provider id is stored on a local author row.

This bites hard after the V3 cutover: the metadata server now answers with `hc:`-keyed author suggestions, while libraries built earlier hold authors added under other provider eras. On my install the completed-download queue filled with items blocked on suggestions for authors that were sitting in the library with the exact suggested `hc:` id (verified in the DB).

## The fix

When `allowAuthorImport` is false, still perform a **lookup-only** resolve of the suggested provider id (new `TryFindExistingSuggestedAuthor`, no add path). If the author exists, run the same author-scoped strict match used by the import-allowed branch. Behaviour for genuinely-new authors is unchanged - nothing is auto-added.

## Tests

New test in `AuthorRestrictedV5RecoveryFixture`: same scenario as the existing `...when_import_allowed` test but with `AllowAuthorImport = false`. It fails on current develop (file lands unmatched with the suggestion) and passes with the fix (links to the existing author, matches the book). Full Core.Test suite green: 2853/2853.

