I tested #294 against a copy of my library: 925 authors, 75,041 audiobooks, 130k editions, in a 650 MB SQLite database. Scheduled tasks were paused, and cover metadata was on disk for every book that has a cover, as on a server that has refreshed its authors. Each run started from a fresh copy of the database. I ran the main request twice per build. The `include=author` row and the no-cover numbers further down come from one run each.

| `GET /api/v1/book?mediaType=audiobook` | develop | #294 |
|---|---:|---:|
| Time | 16.2–16.6 s | 13.8–15.2 s |
| Server peak memory | 1,168 MB | 1,082 MB |
| With `&include=author` (Books table) | 20.9 s, 1,288 MB | 18.3 s, 1,219 MB |

Both builds return the same 75,041 books. `develop` doesn't run out of memory on this library, so the gain here is modest, which fits what you saw at 643k.

**When covers aren't on disk, most of the remaining time goes to the cover proxy.** That's the case on a fresh install or a generated test library, and there the same request takes 96 s on develop and 91 s on #294. Most of that time is in `MediaCoverProxy.RegisterUrl`. It calls `_cache.ClearExpired()` on every call, which walks the whole proxy cache (up to 20,000 entries), so a list of N books walks it N times. Expired entries are already removed by their own timers and on lookup, so the sweep only matters when the cache is full. Moving it inside the full-cache check cut develop from 96 s to 25 s, and the request still returned the same 75,041 books:

```diff
             var hash = url.SHA256Hash();
 
-            _cache.ClearExpired();
-
-            if (_cache.Count >= MaxCacheEntries)
-            {
-                _cache.Clear();
-            }
+            // Entries expire on their own timers and on lookup, so only sweep when the cache is full.
+            if (_cache.Count >= MaxCacheEntries)
+            {
+                _cache.ClearExpired();
+
+                if (_cache.Count >= MaxCacheEntries)
+                {
+                    _cache.Clear();
+                }
+            }
```

The same 20,000-entry cap also breaks covers in large responses. Once one response registers more than 20,000 proxied covers, the cache is cleared partway through it. After one unpaged request (66,534 proxied covers), the cover URLs of the first and middle books returned 404, and only the last one loaded. The same URLs returned 200 once a single-book request registered them again. Paging (#295, #296) makes this much less likely, but it probably deserves its own issue.

**Merging with #295:** the two PRs conflict in `BookRepository.cs` and `BookServiceGetBooksLoadsEditionsFixture.cs`, where both add methods or tests at the same spot. Even after those conflicts are resolved, they don't compile together: #294 makes `targetMediaType` nullable in `GetBooksForDisplay`, so #295's `HasRootFolderForMediaType(targetMediaType)` fails with CS1503. Whichever PR lands second needs `targetMediaType.Value` there.

---
*Measured with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code); I reviewed the results before posting.*

I tested #295 on a copy of my library: 925 authors, 75,041 audiobooks, with the same setup as my #294 comment. The first row below comes from a single run in headless Chromium. The other rows are API timings from two runs each: the shelf's author counts, followed by `/book/shelf` requests for 50 authors at a time, in shelf order.

| `/shelf` | develop | #295 |
|---|---:|---:|
| First books on screen (Chromium) | 20.5 s | 4.2 s |
| First `/book/shelf` request (50 authors, 4,739 books) | n/a | 2.0–2.4 s |
| Whole shelf | 16.2–16.6 s, one request | 27–29 s total, 19 requests |
| Server peak memory | 1,168 MB | 821–829 MB |

All the requests together return the same 75,041 books as develop's single request.

**Bug: shelf rows are cut off after the first line of books.** `CellMeasurer` measures each row while it still shows the loading spinner (38 px). It doesn't measure the row again when the books arrive. On the first screen, all 17 rows had hidden books: 292 of the 345 rendered books were outside their row. Resizing the window shows them, because a resize clears the measurement cache. Measuring the row again once its books load fixed it in my test. No books were hidden on the first screen, or at the two points I checked further down the shelf:

```diff
 // Bookshelf.js, rowRenderer
-        {({ registerChild }) => (
+        {({ measure, registerChild }) => (
 ...
               isSelected={selectedState[item.id]}
+              onBooksLoaded={measure}
```

```diff
 // BookshelfRowConnector.js
   componentDidMount() {
     this.fetchBooksIfNeeded();
+
+    // The row may have been measured while it was still loading.
+    if (this.props.isBooksLoaded) {
+      this.props.onBooksLoaded?.();
+    }
   }
 
-  componentDidUpdate() {
+  componentDidUpdate(prevProps) {
     this.fetchBooksIfNeeded();
+
+    // The row was measured with the loading indicator; measure it again now its books are shown.
+    if (!prevProps.isBooksLoaded && this.props.isBooksLoaded) {
+      this.props.onBooksLoaded?.();
+    }
   }
```

The row connector's propTypes also need `onBooksLoaded: PropTypes.func`.

**Suggestion: load only monitored editions, without `Chapters`.** `GetBooksForBookshelf` calls `GetEditionsByBook`, which loads every edition of those books with all its columns, including `Chapters`, and joins a Book and Author row to each edition. It then keeps only the monitored editions. Loading my whole shelf reads 128,682 editions and 135 MB of `Chapters` JSON, but it uses only 75,041 of those editions and none of the chapters.

I tried a monitored-only query scoped by author that reuses #294's Chapters-free column list, measured on a build with all three PRs (#296 doesn't touch this path). Over all 75,041 books, the JSON was identical (same hash), and:

| | without | with the query below |
|---|---:|---:|
| First request | 2.0–2.2 s | 1.1–1.2 s |
| Whole shelf (19 requests) | 27–28 s | 16 s |
| Server peak memory | 836–850 MB | 584–600 MB |

```csharp
// EditionRepository
public List<Edition> GetMonitoredEditionsForAuthors(IEnumerable<int> authorIds, BookMediaType mediaType)
{
    var ids = authorIds?.Distinct().ToArray() ?? Array.Empty<int>();
    if (ids.Length == 0)
    {
        return new List<Edition>();
    }

    var monitored = true;
    var builder = Builder()
        .Select(_bookListColumns)
        .Join<Edition, Book>((e, b) => e.BookId == b.Id)
        .Where<Edition>(e => e.Monitored == monitored)
        .Where<Book>(b => Enumerable.Contains(ids, b.AuthorId) && b.MediaType == mediaType);

    var sql = builder.AddSelectTemplate(typeof(Edition));
    return _database.Query<Edition>(sql.RawSql, sql.Parameters).ToList();
}
```

`GetBooksForBookshelf` would then call `_editionService.GetMonitoredEditionsForAuthors(authorIds, mediaType)` instead of `GetEditionsByBook(bookIds).Where(e => e.Monitored)`, through a pass-through on `EditionService`. It filters on the request's author IDs (at most 100), so there's nothing to chunk. I haven't run the test suite against it.

When covers aren't on disk, the request that includes the biggest author (5,304 books) takes 17 s. That's the `MediaCoverProxy.RegisterUrl` sweep from my #294 comment. With that sweep fixed, the same request took 4 s.

#294 and #295 also conflict in two files, and they need a one-line fix to compile together; see my #294 comment.

---
*Measured with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code); I reviewed the results before posting.*

